properties.SUMMARY:
THE COMPANY HAS BEEN MANUFACTURING PLASTIC LAMINATES AT THE SITE SINCE 1965. THE PRINCIPLE COMPOUNDS USED ARE PHENOLIC RESINS AND STYRENE. 3 LAGOONS WERE ONSITE FOR RECEIVING WASTEWATER. PAINTS, OILY WASTE, HALOGENS, SOLVENTS, AND PHENOLS WERE DISPOSED IN THE LAGOONS. IN 1976 A SEWER LINE WAS EXTENDED TO THE FACILITY AND THE LAGOONS WERE DRAINED AND FILLED WITH SOIL. THERE HAVE BEEN SEVERAL SPILLS, INCLUDING FUEL OIL, HYDRAULIC OIL, ACETONE, PHENOLS, METHYLCHLORIDE, PCBS, FORMALDEHYDE, PROPYLENE GLYCOL, HYDROCHLORIC ACID, AND POLYMER EMULSION.  ANALYTICAL RESULTS OF GROUND WATER, SEDIMENT, AND SOILS SHOW LOW CONCENTRATIONS OF VOCS, PHTHALATES, PETROLEUM HYDROCARBONS, STYRENE, PAHS AND PHENOLS THROUGHOUT THE SITE. P-413 1991:  DAY TANK OVERFILLED UP THE VENT PIPE AND ONTO THE ROOF.  ROOF DRAINS THEN TOOK THE OIL DOWN PIPING AND OUT INTO THE LITTLE ANDROSCOGGIN RIVER.  SORBENT BOOM AROUND THE OUTLET INTO THE RIVER.  SAWDUST ON ROOF FOR CLEAN UP   - SHERYL SMITH; P-648-1990:  SPILLAGE OF 6 GALLONS OF HYDRAULIC OIL ONTO PAVEMENT AND SOIL SOIL AND GRASS SHOVELED UP ALONG WITH SAND OR PAVEMENTS. - STEVE BREZINSKI; P-226-1985:  SOME OF SPILL MAY HAVE REACHED ANDROSCOGGIN.  SOME OF SPILL LEAKED OIT INTO CRUSHED SOTNE BY THE RAILROAD LOADING RACK.  10 BARRELS OF STONE WERE PICKED UP.  USE OF THE DRAINED STONE IN A DRIVEWAY IN AN URBAN AREA ON CITY WATER. - FRED BRANN; P-331-1992:  CONTAMINATION WAS DISCOVERED DURING A TRENCH EXCAVATION FOR CODING WATER. AN AREA OF ABOUT 15 YDS OF #6 OIL CONTAMINATED SOIL WAS STOCKPILES AND ARRANGEMENTS WERE MADE FOR RECYCLING. GROUNDWATER TECHNOLOGY CONSULTS - SHERYL SMITH;
P-787-1989:  SMALL OIL SPILL INTO THE COMPANYS OUT FALL SOURCE NOT IDENTIFIED BUT COMING FROM THEIR FACILITY. - STEPHEN BREZINSKI; P-220-1984:  PIPE FAILURE UNDER THE FLOOR HAD CAUSED THE SPILL.  THE OIL GOT INTO THE FLOOR DRAIN SYSTEM AND ULTIMATELY INTO THE RIVER.  4 BOOMS SET UP IN THE RIVER. - STEVE EUFEMIA
PLASTICS MANUFACTURER; 1.2 MGD NON-CONTACT COOLING WATER

3. WHILE REMOVING THE GASOLINE AND WATER FROM THE NYNEX UNDERGROUND UTILITY WE OBSERVED LARGE QUANTITIES OF GASOLINE AND WATER ENTERING THE UTILITY VIA A CONDUIT SUGGESTING THE POSSIBLY OF MORE CONTAMINATED NYNEX, UNDERGROUND UTILITIES UP-GRADIENT. WITH ASSISTANCE FROM NYNEX, WE LOCATED THREE ADDITIONAL NYNEX UNDERGROUND UTILITIES. THE NYNEX, UNDERGROUND UTILITY LOCATED DIRECTLY UP-GRADIENT OF THE PREVIOUSLY MENTIONED UTILITY ALSO CONTAINED LARGE QUANTITIES OF GASOLINE WHILE THE OTHER TWO UTILITIES EXHIBITED NO EVIDENCE OF GASOLINE CONTAMINATION. THE LOCATION OF THE OTHER TWO UTILITIES, VOID OF GASOLINE, STRONGLY SUPPORTED THE CONTENTION THAT THE GASOLINE RELEASE HAD ORIGINATED AT B. P. OIL CO. TERMINAL.

TO FURTHER SUPPORT OUR CONTENTION, IT WAS DISCUSSED AND DECIDED UPON THAT A TEST PIT WOULD BE DUG IN CLEAN HARBOR'S STORAGE YARD ADJACENT TO B. P. OIL CO.'S LOADING RACK. AT ABOUT 3' BELOW GRADE, FREE PHASE GASOLINE WAS ENCOUNTERED. AT THIS POINT, IT WAS CLEAR THAT B. P. OIL CO. WAS THE SOURCE OF THE GASOLINE RELEASE, AND ASSUMED RESPONSIBILITY OF THE DISCHARGE.

OVERALL OBJECTIVES FOR THE PROJECT WERE ESTABLISHED AS FOLLOWS:

1. REMOVE NAPL FROM GROUNDWATER;

2. PREVENT MIGRATION OF NAPL AND VAPOR INTO STORM DRAINS, SANITARY SEWERS AND OTHER UTILITIES;

3. PREVENT SHEEN OR PRODUCT FROM ENTERING FORE RIVER;

4. PREVENT OFF-SITE MIGRATION OF CONTAMINATED GROUNDWATERT

